{
  "pre_registration": "STAGED DECOYS, fixed before any footage: for each round and each twin class, the TARGET (the object the engine is briefed to find, described by its attribute word) sits in target_zone and a same-class DECOY of the other attribute sits in decoy_zone, never the same zone. The tip names the decoy's zone: a class-matching commit rule takes the bait. Zone numbers were committed first; the attribute words are filled in from the two objects the owner actually owns, after ONE calibration clip (never graded) and before the decoy sitting is shot.",
  "scope": "This is a PERCEPTION-AND-COGNITION test on real camera pixels, NOT a robot result. Nothing moves: a person carries a phone to six fixed zones in one room and records a short clip of each. The engine chooses which zone's clip to look at next, accumulates OBSERVED and ABSENT_FROM claims, and commits to a zone. No navigation, no manipulation, no autonomy over a physical body is claimed or measured.",
  "generator": "scripts/make_phone_manifest.py --decoys",
  "placement_sha256": "6d892506faca5431adb76993faafe7769a06615709932d0389e79d98267681ab",
  "identity": {
    "disclosure": "Identity on real pixels is an ATTRIBUTE-PROMPT match, not a simulator asset id. YOLO-World is prompted with both '<target attribute> <class>' and '<decoy attribute> <class>' in addition to the fixed vocabulary. Per clip the class's identity is the attribute prompt with the higher maximum confidence (ties go to the decoy); only the target attribute's hit may become an OBSERVED claim about the briefed instance. A bare class hit with neither attribute above the claim gate is a sighting of an anonymous same-class sibling. This is weaker than the hospital decoy protocol's exact instance ids, and it is the strongest identity signal a camera-only pipeline honestly has.",
    "attributes": {
      "Mug": {
